---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Heading from "../components/Heading.astro";

// The privacy policy for mcp.tabnas.dev.
//
// It exists because the hosted endpoint makes a promise in its own
// /.well-known/mcp response — "Document content is never logged, stored, or
// used for training" — and a promise a service makes about itself, published
// nowhere a person can read it, is not a policy. Phase 4's exit criteria ask
// for this page by name.
//
// Keep it matched to what ts/src/worker.ts and ts/src/budget.ts actually do.
// If the telemetry shape changes there, it changes here in the same commit.
const ENDPOINT = "mcp.tabnas.dev";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What the hosted tabnas MCP endpoint records, what it never records, and why running locally avoids the question entirely."
>
  <article class="privacy" data-pagefind-body>
    <Heading level={1} id="privacy">Privacy</Heading>

    <p class="lede">
        This page covers the hosted MCP endpoint at <code>{ENDPOINT}</code>
        and the documentation website at <code>tabnas.dev</code>.
      </p>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="short">The short version</Heading>
      <p>
        The hosted MCP service processes document content in memory and does
        not write it to application logs or persistent storage. It records
        the request metadata listed here. The service does not use
        document content for training.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run the MCP server locally to process documents without sending
        them to the hosted endpoint:
      </p>
      <pre><code>npx --yes @tabnas/mcp mcp</code></pre>
      <p>
        Local stdio processes requests on your machine. Installing the
        package may contact the npm registry.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="recorded">What is recorded</Heading>
      <p>
        Shape only, per request, for keeping the service working:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tool name</strong>: for example <code>parse</code>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>A size bucket</strong>: one of <code>&lt;=1k</code>,
          <code>&lt;=16k</code>, <code>&lt;=64k</code>,
          <code>&lt;=256k</code>. Deliberately a bucket and never a byte
          count: an exact document length is a weak fingerprint of the
          document, and this service does not hold facts about content.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Duration</strong> in milliseconds.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Status and error code</strong>: whether the tool answered
          yes or no, and which documented code it returned.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The service records these fields for each request. Its telemetry
        tests check that parsed content does not appear in an emitted record.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="never">What is never recorded</Heading>
      <ul>
        <li>The documents, grammars, fixtures or options you send.</li>
        <li>The trees, diagnostics or reports returned to you.</li>
        <li>Any byte of request or response body, in any form.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The application does not retain request or response bodies.
        Cloudflare handles requests in transit as described in the next section.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="cloudflare">What Cloudflare sees</Heading>
      <p>
        The endpoint runs on Cloudflare Workers, so Cloudflare operates the
        network path and applies its own standard protections. Being the
        network, it necessarily handles your request in transit and sees the
        connecting IP address. Per-IP rate limiting uses that address to
        count requests; the count is what is kept, not a log of who you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare&rsquo;s
        privacy policy</a> for its handling of network request data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="site">The website</Heading>
      <p>
        <code>tabnas.dev</code> is a static site. No analytics, no tracking
        pixels, no advertising, no cookies set by us. Search runs entirely in
        your browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={2} id="changes">Changes</Heading>
      <p>
        The website and MCP service have separate repositories. Update this
        page when the service&rsquo;s data handling changes. Their
        <a href="https://github.com/tabnas">source repositories</a> contain
        the change history.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
